---
import Shell from "../layouts/Shell.astro"
import { catalog } from "../../src/model/catalog.ts"
import { SITE } from "../lib/site.ts"

const data = await catalog()
---

<Shell title="About" description={`How ${SITE.name} is built and what it does and does not cover.`}>
	<h1 class="m-0 text-[1.7rem] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">About</h1>

	<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-5 max-w-[68ch] text-[15px]">
		<p class="m-0">
			The OpenTelemetry semantic conventions are published as a set of YAML files in which every
			attribute carries a stability level and, when deprecated, the name of whatever replaced it.
			That makes the registry diffable. This site diffs it, release by release, and turns the result
			into pages you can link to.
		</p>

		<h2 class="m-0 mt-3 text-[1.05rem] font-semibold">How it is built</h2>
		<p class="m-0">
			Every tagged release from v{data.versions.at(-1)} onward is downloaded, normalized into a flat
			snapshot of attributes, metrics and signals, and committed. The site builds from those
			committed snapshots with no network access, so a page can always be reproduced from the
			repository alone. A scheduled job checks for new releases and opens a pull request when it
			finds one.
		</p>
		<p class="m-0">
			The conventions are mid-migration between two model file formats. Both are read. This matters
			more than it sounds: v1.44.0 moved <code class="font-mono text-[0.92em]">server.*</code>,
			<code class="font-mono text-[0.92em]">client.*</code> and every
			<code class="font-mono text-[0.92em]">hw.*</code> metric to the newer format, and a reader that
			understood only the older one would report all of them as deleted from the registry.
		</p>

		<h2 class="m-0 mt-3 text-[1.05rem] font-semibold">What "breaking" means here</h2>
		<p class="m-0">
			A change is marked breaking when it removes, renames, or walks back something the project had
			already declared stable or release&#8209;candidate &mdash; the tier where the conventions
			promise not to do that. The same change to a development-stability definition is marked
			notable instead, because churn there is expected and flagging it as breaking would drown the
			signal. Upstream release notes use their own categories; both are shown, and they do not
			always agree.
		</p>

		<h2 class="m-0 mt-3 text-[1.05rem] font-semibold">Limits</h2>
		<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
			<li>
				History starts at v{data.versions.at(-1)}. An attribute described as first seen there may be
				much older.
			</li>
			<li>
				Only the conventions registry is covered so far &mdash; not the OpenTelemetry specification
				prose or the OTLP protocol definitions.
			</li>
			<li>
				Definitions are reported as the registry states them. Where the registry is silent, so is
				this site.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 class="m-0 mt-3 text-[1.05rem] font-semibold">For agents</h2>
		<p class="m-0">
			Every page has a machine-readable counterpart. See <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> for the map,
			or fetch <a href="/api/attributes.json" class="font-mono text-[0.92em]">/api/attributes.json</a>
			directly.
		</p>

		<p class="m-0 text-muted text-[13.5px] mt-3">
			Independent project, built by <a href={SITE.builderUrl}>{SITE.builder}</a>. Not affiliated with
			the OpenTelemetry project, the CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Source data is licensed by the
			OpenTelemetry Authors under Apache 2.0.
		</p>
	</div>
</Shell>
